      "concern": "The title and physical_scope claim forward initial-value uniqueness, and proof step 5.1 begins 'For the homogeneous difference of two solutions with equal data at t0, linearity gives J=0'. However, the impedance admissibility condition in def-emx-finite-energy-boundary-model is only a quadratic scalar Green identity for each solution and its separately supplied boundary pair. It supplies neither a linear trace map nor cross Green identities, so differences need not be admissible. A concrete counterexample uses Ω=(0,1)^3, I=(-1/4,1/4), and coefficients with SI numerical values ε=μ=Y=1. Put f(s)=1 for s≥0 and f(s)=1−(1/2)exp(−1/s²) for s<0. Both E=e_y,H=e_z and E=f(x−t)e_y,H=f(x−t)e_z solve the homogeneous bulk equations and agree at t=0, but differ for t>0. The second has nonnegative total outward power P(t)=f(1−t)²−f(−t)². Give it the artificial boundary data Eτ=√P(t)e_y on the x=1 face and zero elsewhere, with Hτ=n×Eτ; these satisfy exactly the stipulated Green identity and impedance law. Give the constant solution zero boundary data. Both are admissible under the written definition. Thus general impedance initial-value uniqueness is false for this class. The stated energy identities and the explicitly narrower zero-initial-data conclusion remain valid.",
